{"context":"EKA v1.1 keeps three different conventions in play: Markdown authoring with YAML frontmatter (kebab/snake case), the RSF serialization (mixed kebab/snake), and the machine interface `eka-cko-v1` (kebab). Every layer requires its own parsing and naming map, and external tools must parse Markdown to reach structured content. The standard documents the model, not a format (ADR-012 §Decision 3: the runtime consumes CKO only; authoring is an adapter), so the authoring format is a convention decision, not a model decision.\n\nTwo forces converge. First, WYSIWYG: an author writing a Markdown file cannot see what `eka get` returns — the machine document renames every field. Second, structured content: nested knowledge (alternatives tables, section objects) cannot be expressed in the one-level Markdown section convention at all. The v2.0 milestone replaces the authoring, serialization and machine conventions with ONE naming convention — camelCase — and makes JSON the native authoring encoding, with Markdown retained as a legacy adapter (spec-standard-v2, proposed; ratified by this ADR).","decision":"1. **EKA Standard v2.0: camelCase everywhere the standard is visible.** Authoring JSON files, the RSF v2.0 serialization (unit.json, manifest, header, declarations, integrity) and the machine interface (`eka-cko-v2`) all use lower camelCase keys (§2 derivation: content sections map deterministically — `Alternatives Considered` → `alternativesConsidered`, `Acceptance Criteria` → `acceptanceCriteria`, `Projected Status` → `projectedStatus`, `Change Log` → `changeLog`). **State VALUES are unchanged** (lowercase: `accepted`, `in-progress`) — only keys are camelCase. The reference grammar (`\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e[:\u003cv\u003e]`) is unchanged. WYSIWYG: what an author writes is what `eka get` returns.\n\n2. **JSON-native authoring with strict schema.** One artifact = one file `docs/\u003cdimension\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e-\u003cid\u003e.json` (§3.2): identity (`namespace`, `type`, `id`, `instanceVersion`, `revision`), metadata, `state` object, classification, `relationships` object, `changeLog` array (camelCase domain names), and the `content` object — the type's required section keys plus allowed extra keys, values strings or nested objects. Unknown top-level fields are rejected (strict schema, same philosophy as RSF reject-by-default). Rule 9 becomes a content-key check. The dimension folder rule (R6) and the filename-as-projection rule (R2, P3) are unchanged.\n\n3. **Structured content with canonical encoding.** Content is a JSON object — `eka/structured-json/1` — encoded canonically (§3.3): required section keys in registry order first, extra keys lexicographic, nested object keys sorted, compact, no trailing newline. The compiler owns the canonicalization: identical knowledge always yields identical payload bytes, so digests stay deterministic. `eka/structured-text/1` remains readable (legacy payloads in the store, legacy packages at import); Markdown authoring compiles into the structured content object (§6: sections → camelCase keys, extra headings allowed, preamble dropped — the projection header line of tkt-/ctr- artifacts is retained as the first line of the first content key, because rule 8's marker is structural, not knowledge content).\n\n4. **RSF v2.0 with legacy import.** All serialized keys switch to camelCase (§4 full mapping); `SerializationVersion = \"2\"`. Packages declaring `\"1.1\"` (or the pre-1.1 `\"1\"`) import through a JSON key-rename pass (one decode path, two key spellings) and re-emit as v2.0; exports always emit v2.0. Package structure, digest semantics (`SHA-256(unit.json ‖ content)`) and determinism contracts are unchanged. The workspace SQLite schema stays v3 — content is an opaque BLOB, no migration; legacy structured-text payloads stay readable.\n\n5. **Machine interface `eka-cko-v2`.** The schema string becomes `eka-cko-v2` and the document keys follow the same camelCase convention (`canonicalForm`, `engineeringDomain`, `stateVector`, `instanceVersion`, `dimensionsSecondary`, `changeLog`, `objectHash`). Content for structured-json is `{representation, fields}` — the content object verbatim; legacy structured-text stays `{representation, text}`. `eka-cko-v1` is not emitted anymore (breaking, accepted: no external consumers; ADR-015's schema-bump rule).\n\n6. **Drafts and inline publish are JSON-native.** `eka new` scaffolds `\u003ctype\u003e-\u003cid\u003e.json` (identity + owned-state defaults + required content keys + change-log with the current date); `--content-file` accepts a JSON object merged into the content (raw text rejected); `PublishInline` input follows the §3.2 schema. Legacy Markdown drafts remain readable and publishable (their bodies compile to structured content).\n\n7. **Migration without data loss.** v1.1 Markdown repositories keep validating and synchronizing (content compiled to JSON); the reference project migrates to JSON-native authoring (proof of the format, 37 artifacts — identities/state/relationships unchanged; snapshot regenerated, new digests accepted). This repository's own ADRs (reference/decisions/) stay Markdown on purpose — they prove the backward-compatibility path. Standard documents gain v2.0 companions; the v1.1 documents remain as historical records. CLI artifact version line unchanged (0.2.x); `standardVersion = \"2.0\"` (validator output \"EKA v2.0\").","consequences":"- **One naming convention end to end.** Authoring, serialization and machine output agree; no layer-specific naming maps. WYSIWYG removes the mental translation cost for authors and tooling.\n- **Nested content becomes expressible** (JSON-native authoring); Markdown compilation produces one level (sections) — documented limitation of the legacy adapter.\n- **Digests of all future snapshots change** (content payload bytes differ) — accepted, no external consumers. v1.1 snapshots and Markdown artifacts become legacy: supported indefinitely for import/validation, never emitted.\n- **Strict JSON schema** rejects unknown top-level fields and malformed content at the gate — the authoring adapter is as strict as the RSF importer.\n- **Breaking for machine consumers**: `eka-cko-v1` documents are no longer emitted. Accepted under ADR-015's schema-bump rule (no external consumers).\n- **Import writer follows the representation**: structured-json units import as `.json` authoring files; legacy structured-text units (v1.1 packages) still import as `.md`.\n\n### Addendum — EKA Standard v2.0 implementation (2026-08-09)\n\n*This addendum records the implementation clarifications applied during the v2.0 milestone (spec-standard-v2 §12 checklist):*\n\n- **JSON authoring carries `instanceVersion`** for repository artifacts (required, mirroring the v1.1 frontmatter contract — identity is authoritative, P3, and R1/R2 need the version); drafts carry none (assigned at publish).\n- **Projection header retention**: the tkt-/ctr- preamble's exact projection header line is retained as the first line of the first compiled content key (rule 8 marker), preserving re-validation and the export → import → export round trip.\n- **`eka get` content.fields** carries the canonical payload bytes re-indented with the document (json.Indent over the emitted document) — key order preserved, determinism intact.\n- **The import writer emits `.json` authoring files** for structured-json units and `.md` for legacy structured-text units (v1.1 imports stay Markdown-shaped).\n- **`DecodeUnit` is legacy-aware** (key-presence sniff + rename): the store may hold v1.1 unit.json bytes from older snapshots (opaque BLOB, no migration); push and integrity paths reconstruct and re-emit them as v2.0.\n\n### References\n\n- [reference/spec-standard-v2.md](../spec-standard-v2.md) — the v2.0 convention document (design basis).\n- [standard/eka-specification-v2.0.md](../../standard/eka-specification-v2.0.md) — the ratified v2.0 standard amendment.\n- [ADR-012](adr-012-canonical-knowledge-object-runtime.md) — CKO runtime (amended: content representation).\n- [ADR-015](adr-015-machine-retrieval-interface.md) — machine interface (amended: schema v2).\n- [Migration report addendum 14](../migration-report-runtime-v0.2.0.md#14-addendum-eka-standard-v20-json-native-authoring) — the v2.0 migration record.","alternativesConsidered":"- **Keep Markdown as the only authoring format** (v1.1 unchanged): rejected — nested content impossible, WYSIWYG broken, external tooling must parse Markdown.\n- **Snake_case or kebab-case JSON**: rejected — the point is ONE convention; JSON's ecosystem idiom is camelCase, and the single-word keys stay identical either way.\n- **In-place amendment of the v1.1 standard documents**: rejected — the v1.1 documents remain as historical records; v2.0 gets its own documents (the amendment over the v1.1 corpus), keeping the ratification trail readable.\n- **In-place migration of this repository's ADRs**: rejected — the ADRs stay Markdown to prove the legacy adapter works on the most important corpus: the implementation's own decisions."}